WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:25.860
<v Ira Glass>Back when Cliff worked as a room service waiter at Holiday Inn, so many guests ordered in pizza delivery to avoid the lousy room service. But sometimes the food and beverage manager, who was this tightly-wound guy and immigrant from Algeria, would stake out the lobby and try to catch the pizza delivery guys and kick them out before they made it to the elevators. Then one day this food and beverage manager came up with a better plan-- to trick the guests.
<v Ira Glass>当时克里夫在假日酒店当客房送餐服务员，很多客人都叫比萨外卖，以避开那糟糕的客房送餐服务。但有时食品与饮料部经理，那个精神高度紧张的阿尔及利亚移民，会埋伏在大堂，试图抓到比萨外送员，在他们到达电梯前就把他们赶出去。后来有一天，这位餐饮经理想出了一个更好的计划——欺骗客人。

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:41.560
<v Ira Glass>The hotel got some cardboard pizza boxes and takeout menus that looked like they could've come from any delivery places you've ever seen. Each of them had a cartoon of an Italian chef with a moustache and a big hat. He was holding his fingers to his lips in the classic kind of "Mama mia, that's delicious" sort of pose.
<v Ira Glass>酒店弄到了一些硬纸板比萨盒和外卖菜单，看起来就像你见过的任何外卖店的那样。每一个盒子上都印着一个留着胡子、戴着大帽子的意大利厨师的卡通画。他把手指放在嘴唇上，摆出经典的“妈妈咪呀，太好吃了”那种姿势。

00:00:41.560 --> 00:01:06.820
<v Ira Glass>This pizza place had a name-- Giorgio's-- but the menus didn't list any address. They did list a phone number. And the phone number was not an extension in the hotel phone system. It wasn't a room service number. It rang at this red telephone, an outside line that they installed on the basement wall right near the big institutional hotel switchboard that the room service operators normally use to take orders.
<v Ira Glass>这家比萨店有个名字——乔治奥（Giorgio's）——但菜单上没有列出任何地址。它们确实列出了一个电话号码。而这个电话号码不是酒店电话系统里的分机。它也不是客房送餐服务电话。它响的是一部红色的电话，那是他们安装在地下室墙上的一条外线，就在客房送餐服务员通常用来接订单的酒店大型机构化总机旁边。

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:16.610
<v Cliff Doerksen>And to complete illusion, we were given stacks of-- you know those green checks that you get in diners, the waitress just fills it out by hand?
<v Cliff Doerksen>为了让骗局更逼真，我们收到了一叠——你知道那种你在小餐馆里拿到的绿色账单吗？就是服务员用手填的那种？

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:27.600
<v Ira Glass>This is Cliff. So we were supposed to fill one of those out and tape it onto the box for just that finishing touch of verisimilitude.
<v Ira Glass>这是克里夫。所以我们本应填好其中一张，贴在盒子上，作为最后的画龙点睛之笔，增加真实感。

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.490
<v Ira Glass>And so how would it work? Somebody would call the number, and then did you have to answer the phone and say "Giorgio's"?
<v Ira Glass>那么它是如何运作的呢？有人会拨打那个号码，然后你们必须接电话并说“乔治奥”吗？

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:56.460
<v Cliff Doerksen>Yeah. We soon realized that being an outside line, we were relieved from the standards of ordinary hotel courtesy. So it was all this, "Giorgio pizza!" It was like a platform for improv. Use these ridiculous sort of Vaudeville Italian accents. "What's the matter for you? Hey Louie, where's the driver?"
<v Cliff Doerksen>是的。我们很快意识到，作为一条外线，我们不必遵守酒店常规礼仪的标准。所以我们就会说：“乔治奥比萨！”这就像是一个即兴表演的平台。我们用那些滑稽的歌舞杂耍般的意大利口音说：“你怎么回事？嘿，路易，送货员在哪儿？”

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:02.240
<v Ira Glass>I want to apologize to any Italian-American listeners right now on behalf of Public Radio in general.
<v Ira Glass>我现在谨代表公共广播电台，向所有意大利裔美国听众道歉。

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:12.420
<v Cliff Doerksen>Absolutely unforgivable on any level.
<v Cliff Doerksen>在任何层面上这绝对都是不可原谅的。

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:22.990
<v Ira Glass>A room service waiter named Kevin had a character named Luigi that he would play on the one. The captain of the room service waiters, Brandon, been a loyal company man until all this absurdity began.
<v Ira Glass>一个叫凯文的客房送餐服务员有一个叫路易吉的角色，他会在电话里扮演这个角色。客房送餐服务主管布兰登，在所有这些荒唐事开始之前，一直是个忠诚的员工。

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:36.120
<v Cliff Doerksen>And he could do a really excellent sort of Sylvester Stallone croak, which I cannot. And he could also do Brando doing The Godfather, which he loved to do. And, you know, mumbling--
<v Cliff Doerksen>他能模仿那种西尔维斯特·史泰龙式的沙哑嗓音，我可学不来。他还能模仿马龙·白兰度在《教父》里的样子，这是他最喜欢做的。你知道，就是那种嘟囔着说话——

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.050
<v Cliff Doerksen>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Cliff Doerksen>[交谈声]

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:41.420
<v Ira Glass>And so, all right. So if I'm calling for a pizza and I say, like, hi, I want to get like a--
<v Ira Glass>好吧。如果我打电话叫比萨，我说，嘿，我想来一份……

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:50.820
<v Cliff Doerksen>You're getting a serious runaround, is what you're getting.
<v Cliff Doerksen>你会遇到严重的推诿，这就是你会得到的待遇。

00:02:50.820 --> 00:03:07.800
<v Ira Glass>There were two flaws in the deceit, and they were pretty big flaws, Cliff says. One was the pizzas themselves. They were frozen, and they were the size of room service pizzas, not real pizzas from a pizzeria. Smallest just nine inches. A large was the size of an old vinyl LP.
<v Ira Glass>克里夫说，这个骗局有两个缺陷，而且是相当大的缺陷。一个是比萨本身。它们是冷冻的，大小和客房送餐的比萨一样，而不是真正的比萨店出的比萨。最小的只有九英寸。大的只有一张旧黑胶唱片那么大。

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:24.220
<v Ira Glass>The other flaw was the delivery outfit. Conceivably, the room service waiters could have just changed out of their polyester tux jackets and bow ties back into street clothes and then pretended to be pizza delivery guys. Or they could have stayed in uniform and told the guests that they had intercepted the pizza on the way up.
<v Ira Glass>另一个缺陷是送货装束。可以想象，客房送餐服务员本可以脱掉涤纶燕尾服夹克和领结，换回便装，然后假装成比萨外送员。或者他们可以穿着制服，告诉客人在送餐途中拦截了比萨。

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:27.860
<v Ira Glass>Instead of either of those options, though, the food and beverage manager chose--
<v Ira Glass>然而，餐饮经理没有选择这两个选项中的任何一个，而是选择了——

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:49.310
<v Cliff Doerksen>A very strange garment, the like I've never seen before or since, which was sort of a kind of peppermint-striped apron. White with vertical red stripes. And it was huge. It was enormous. I was absolutely, you know, lost in this bizarre garment. And it came with a floppy hat made of the same basic fabric.
<v Cliff Doerksen>一种非常奇怪的服装，我这辈子都没见过，那是一种薄荷条纹的围裙。白色，带有垂直的红色条纹。而且它很大。真是巨大。我完全迷失在这件古怪的衣服里了。它还配有一顶由同样基本布料制成的软帽。

00:03:49.310 --> 00:04:10.610
<v Cliff Doerksen>None of it looked like anything any pizza delivery guy in this hemisphere had ever worn. And all sort of protested that this is a terrible flaw in your illusion. Because we had just been in the room. The waiter, you'd go up and you'd deliver a six pack of beer. And then 20 minutes later, you'd be back with the same face--
<v Cliff Doerksen>这看起来一点也不像这个半球任何比萨外送员穿过的衣服。大家都在抗议，说这是你骗局中的一个严重缺陷。因为我们刚去过客房。送餐员刚上去送过六瓶啤酒。然后20分钟后，你又带着同一张脸回来——

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:13.360
<v Ira Glass>To the same room?
<v Ira Glass>去同一个房间？

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:40.720
<v Cliff Doerksen>But dressed as this-- I always thought of it as a rodeo clown costume. If you'd send me in as a rodeo clown when I'd just been there as a room service waiter, it's going to stimulate suspicion. Plus, it was humiliating to wear this ridiculous getup. And then it was so stupid. It was just done so badly.
<v Cliff Doerksen>但打扮成这样——我一直认为这是马戏团小丑的服装。如果你在我刚以客房服务员的身份去过那个房间后，又派我以马戏团小丑的身份去，这会引起怀疑。而且，穿这套荒唐的行头简直是羞辱。这也太蠢了。做得太糟糕了。

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:50.530
<v Ira Glass>No matter if it was done badly. It was done just well enough, with just enough of the details right, the menus, the outside phone line, that it made lots of money, right from the start.
<v Ira Glass>无论做得多么糟糕。只要做得足够好，细节足够到位，比如菜单、外线电话，它从一开始就赚了很多钱。

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:55.990
<v Ira Glass>Did guests ever say anything indicating that they were onto the scam?
<v Ira Glass>客人有没有说过什么表明他们识破了骗局的话？

00:04:55.990 --> 00:05:26.070
<v Cliff Doerksen>People gave you weird looks. But only once did somebody completely tip their hand, and did somebody ask me. The guy, he's busy signing his name on the check. And he says, "So." And he sort of gives me a smile. He's not being confrontational. He's being sort of sardonic. And he says, "So just exactly where is Giorgio's Pizza, anyway?"
<v Cliff Doerksen>人们会用奇怪的眼神看你。但只有一次，有人完全摊牌了，问了我。那个家伙正忙着在账单上签名。他说：“那么。”他对我笑了笑。他不是在对峙。他表现得有点讽刺。他说：“那么，乔治奥比萨店到底在哪里？”

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:49.160
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our show, bait and switch. We have four stories. In some of these stories are people running the scam running the bait and switch, are unrepentant. In some they feel guilty, they feel contrite. But in all of theses scams, once you know it's a scam, you can't believe people try to pull it off with a straight face.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台，《美国生活》节目，由公共广播国际公司发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天我们的节目主题是“诱饵与转换”。我们有四个故事。在其中一些故事里，实施这种“诱饵与转换”骗局的人并没有悔意。在另一些故事里，他们感到内疚，感到忏悔。但在所有这些骗局中，一旦你知道这是一个骗局，你简直无法相信人们居然试图面不改色地把这事做下去。

00:05:49.160 --> 00:06:05.270
<v Ira Glass>Our four acts involve a car, a fake valentine, a fake girlfriend, and treating people for the Lord Almighty. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>我们的四个篇章涉及一辆车、一张假情人节卡片、一个假女友，以及以上帝的名义对待他人。请继续收听。

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:07.980
<v Ira Glass>Act One Neighborhood Watch.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕：社区守望。

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:15.570
<v Ira Glass>We begin in Austin, Texas, with some people who want nothing more than the best for all concerned. Michael May tells what happened.
<v Ira Glass>我们从德克萨斯州奥斯汀开始，那里有一些人，他们一心只为所有人着想。迈克尔·梅讲述了发生的事情。

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:35.540
<v Ira Glass>Mark Douglas Ledford is 39 years old and plays in several punk rock bands. But don't let that give you the wrong impression. He's a Good Samaritan kind of guy. A tattoo across his chest reads, "To whom much is given, much is required." He owns his own home and has a day job. November 29, 2007 started off like any other work day.
<v Ira Glass>马克·道格拉斯·莱德福德今年39岁，在几支朋克摇滚乐队里演奏。但别让这些给你留下错误的印象。他是个好撒玛利亚人类型的人。他胸前纹着一行字：“多给谁，就向谁多要。”他有自己的房子，也有份日常工作。2007年11月29日，像往常的工作日一样开始了。

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:58.190
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>It was a Thursday morning. I went to work. I came home, I pulled into the driveway, and there was a car parked on the curb near my driveway. I noticed that the windows were down, and the doors were unlocked, and the keys were in the ignition, and it was kind of a big dangly keychain. Like it looked like a girl's keychain. It had a bunch of stuff hanging off of it.
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>那是一个周四的早上。我去上班。回家后，我把车开进车道，路边停着一辆车。我注意到车窗是开着的，门没锁，钥匙插在点火开关上，而且那是一串看起来很晃眼的钥匙链。看起来像个女生的钥匙链。上面挂着一堆东西。

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:10.280
<v Michael May>Something was off. Mark lives a few miles north of downtown in a ranch home that sits on a corner of a quiet street with only one house nearby. The only people who park on this stretch are Mark's bandmates when they come over to practice.
<v Michael May>有些不对劲。马克住在市中心以北几英里的地方，那是一栋平房，坐落在一个安静街道的拐角处，附近只有一栋房子。在这个路段停车的唯一人就是马克的乐队成员，他们来排练时会停在这里。

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:24.470
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>And so I immediately walked across the street to my neighbor Sarah's house. And I knocked on the door, and I said, hey, do you have company over? I think they left the keys in the ignition over there. And she said, I don't know whose car that is.
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>所以我立刻穿过马路走到邻居萨拉家。我敲了敲门，说：“嘿，你有客人吗？我觉得他们的钥匙插在点火开关上。”她说：“我不知道那是谁的车。”

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:41.640
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>And when I went back by and looked at it again, it looked to me like something had happened. Like someone left their car abruptly. And I thought it was suspicious enough, I went in and I dialed 911.
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>当我回去再看一眼时，觉得好像发生了什么事。就像有人匆忙离开了他们的车。我觉得这足够可疑，就进去拨打了911。

00:07:41.640 --> 00:08:03.420
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>It was not even like 10 minutes, and there was a knock at the door, and it was a police officer. They said, did you report this car? And I said, yeah, isn't that crazy? And they said, well, what's the problem? It's legally parked. And I said, well, the windows are down and the key's in ignition. And they said, well, you know, you'd be surprised what we see out here.
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>不到10分钟，就有人敲门，是个警察。他们说：“是你报告了这辆车吗？”我说：“是啊，这不奇怪吗？”他们说：“好吧，有什么问题吗？它是合法停放的。”我说：“好吧，车窗是开着的，钥匙插在点火开关里。”他们说：“好吧，你知道，我们在这里见怪不怪了。”

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:17.720
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>And I was just like, are you kidding me? And so my thought was, OK. Well, maybe it doesn't seem strange to you, but it seems strange to me. Can you contact the owner or can you do something? Because they seemed just reluctant to do anything.
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>我当时就想，你在跟我开玩笑吗？所以我的想法是，好吧。也许这对你来说不奇怪，但对我来说很奇怪。你能联系车主或者做点什么吗？因为他们似乎很不愿意做任何事。

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:26.250
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>And the officers' response at that time was, well, you know. People, they buy these cars and they don't get them registered properly. I guess I could do a cross check on it.
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>警察当时的回答是，嗯，你知道。人们买了这些车，却没有适当地注册。我想我可以查一下。

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:41.010
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>And at that point, I was frustrated. And I said, listen. I don't know what your procedure is. They say if you see suspicious things in your neighborhood, report them. I'm reporting it. And there you go. And the officer went back to their car and drove off.
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>在那一刻，我很沮丧。我说：“听着。我不知道你们的程序是什么。他们说如果你在社区看到可疑的事情，就报告它们。我正在报告。就是这样。”警察回到了他们的车里开车走了。

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:51.730
<v Asia Ward>There's women's clothing in the backseat. And not just like women's, but like, a women's, like-- they looked like stripper clothes, and then men's work boots and rope.
<v Asia Ward>后座有女性的衣服。而且不仅仅是女性的，看起来就像是脱衣舞娘的衣服，还有男士的工作靴和绳子。

00:08:51.730 --> 00:09:05.210
<v Michael May>This is Mark's girlfriend at the time, Asia Ward. She's younger than Mark, 21. A cute gothy girl with a thing for zombie movies. Likes to read books about serial killers. They both started to imagine all sorts of sinister scenarios.
<v Michael May>这是马克当时的女友，亚洲·沃德。她比马克小，21岁。一个可爱的哥特女孩，对僵尸电影情有独钟。喜欢读关于连环杀手的书。他们俩开始想象各种险恶的场景。

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:38.820
<v Asia Ward>Like some poor little stripper lady was taken from her work and like murdered, and this is her car. Because if that cop walked by, even if they weren't going to do anything about it, they could have at least been like, oh, yeah. Get right on that. And like, made us feel better about it, you know? Or said that they were going to investigate it, or looked at it, been like, yeah, this does seem kind of weird. Because it does! Like, if you think that that doesn't seem weird then there's something wrong with you! You know? That's a fishy situation right there.
<v Asia Ward>比如某个可怜的小脱衣舞娘在工作时被带走并谋杀了，这就是她的车。因为如果那个警察走过来，即使他们不想做任何事，他们至少可以像，“哦，是啊，马上处理。”让你感觉好一点，你知道吗？或者说他们会去调查，或者看一眼，说“是的，这看起来确实有点怪。”因为确实如此！就像，如果你觉得这不怪，那你就有什么问题！你知道吗？这情况真可疑。

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:51.160
<v Michael May>By the next day, Friday, neighbors are trying to worry, too, about the car. Sarah, the woman who lives across the street, even calls the cops again and sends Mark a text to let him know she's on the case.
<v Michael May>第二天周五，邻居们也开始担心这辆车了。住马路对面的萨拉甚至又报了警，并给马克发了短信，让他知道她在跟进这事。

00:09:51.160 --> 00:10:01.120
<v Michael May>That night, Mark goes to play a gig in San Antonio. And when he and Asia roll back home around midnight, there's the car, still just sitting there with the keys dangling from the ignition.
<v Michael May>那天晚上，马克去圣安东尼奥演出。当他和亚洲在午夜左右开车回家时，那辆车依然停在那里，钥匙晃晃悠悠地插在点火开关上。

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:14.630
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>So when I came home Saturday night, I expected the car to be gone. I expected them to have towed it away. And at that point, I was just like-- I had it. I was like, OK. We're going to figure out what's going on with this car. That's it.
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>所以当我周六晚上回家时，我以为车已经开走了。我以为他们已经把车拖走了。在那一刻，我只是觉得——我受够了。我当时想，好吧。我们要弄清楚这辆车到底是怎么回事。就是这样。

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:25.260
<v Asia Ward>And I was like, well, there has to be insurance papers, business card, something in the car that'll let us know who the car belongs to, and maybe we can call them and tell them, your car's parked here.
<v Asia Ward>我当时想，车里一定有保险文件、名片或者什么东西，能让我们知道这车是谁的，也许我们可以打电话告诉他们，你的车停在这儿。

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:44.670
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>And at this point we're thinking, there might be a dead body, someone bound and gagged, it's a crime scene. And I'm like, well, I don't want to put my fingerprints all over a situation like that. So I put some gloves on. I consciously go, I'm going to put gloves on so I don't leave fingerprints. And they're black knit gloves, and they have like a little skeleton screenprint on them.
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>当时我们心想，可能里面有具尸体，有人被绑住塞住了嘴，这是个犯罪现场。我当时想，我可不想在这种情况下留下我的指纹。所以我戴上了一些手套。我清醒地想，我要戴上手套，这样我就不会留下指纹。那是黑色的针织手套，上面印着一个小骷髅。

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:59.610
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>So I get the gloves, and I open the door, and I start going through the glove box. And I didn't see anything. And I was talking to Asia. She's standing on the sidewalk. And I'm saying, there's nothing in the glove box. I go through the console. And then I would get out of the car and close the door.
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>我拿上手套，打开车门，开始翻手套箱。我什么也没看见。我当时在和亚洲说话。她站在人行道上。我说，手套箱里什么都没有。我查看了中控台。然后我会下车关上门。

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:14.900
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>And then we were talking, well, should I go in the truck? OK, go in the trunk. And so I'd open the door again, and I'd go back in the car, and I got the keys out of the ignition. And I tried to open the trunk, and it was like when I'd stick the key in, it was like there's something jammed in the lock.
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>然后我们还在讨论，我应该去后备箱看看吗？好吧，去后备箱。所以我又打开车门，回到车里，把钥匙从点火开关上拔了下来。我试着打开后备箱，感觉当我插入钥匙时，好像有什么东西卡在锁里了。

00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:24.900
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>And I was like, listen. I'm going to get a screwdriver. I'm going to open the trunk. If I break somebody's lock, I'll pay them back. You know, whatever. We're just going to get to the bottom of what's going on here.
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>我当时想，听着。我要拿个螺丝刀。我要打开后备箱。如果我弄坏了别人的锁，我会赔偿的。你知道，随便吧。我们只是要弄清楚这里到底发生了什么。

00:11:24.900 --> 00:11:32.980
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>And you know, I mean, I don't know what to do. I just stuck the flat blade screwdriver in the keyhole tried to turn it, and it wouldn't do anything.
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>你知道，我不知道该怎么办。我只是把平口螺丝刀插进钥匙孔试着转动，但它一点反应都没有。

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:41.350
<v Asia Ward>So he puts the screwdriver down and gets back into the car, and is digging through the center console. And he looks out the window, and he's just like, hey, Asia, is that a cop?
<v Asia Ward>所以他放下螺丝刀回到车里，开始翻中控台。他向窗外看去，说：“嘿，亚洲，那是警察吗？”

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:45.010
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>And Asia was like, no. And I go, that sure looks like a cop.
<v Mark Douglas Ledford>亚洲说：“不。”我说：“那看起来确实像警察。”

